WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:12.429
Ashley Christian: Hey everyone, and welcome to the number 2 septic Podcast. Today, we have Kate Carney on, and she is a professional engineer… wait, did I say that right?

2
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.730
Ashley Christian: Professional engineer, okay, I'm gonna just restart, sorry.

3
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:22.760
Ashley Christian: Professional engineer, that's how… that's your title, right? Yep. Okay. I'm just gonna restart.

4
00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:39.550
Ashley Christian: Hey everyone, welcome to the Number 2 Septic Podcast. Today, I have Kate Carney, and she is a professional engineer, which is the first time we've ever had an on-site engineer on the podcast, so I'm really excited to have her on and talk about things from a different perspective. So, Kate, welcome to the podcast.

5
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:40.810
Kate: Thank you.

6
00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:56.030
Ashley Christian: So, Kate and I met recently, and she is the incoming vice president of NOWERA, and, she has a lot of experience in designing systems, both commercial and residential,

7
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.539
Ashley Christian: So that's kind of her credentials, but Kate, why don't you tell us a little bit about

8
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:17.599
Ashley Christian: how you got started, I know you got your degree, in biological sciences and, environmental sciences and engineering. When you got your degree, were you thinking, I want to go into the on-site wastewater industry, or how did that whole thing kind of play out for you?

9
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:28.849
Kate: So, when I got my degree, my master's thesis was on, the effects of binary metal mixtures on cereodaphnia dubia, which are water fleas.

10
00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:40.959
Kate: So, kind of aquatic toxicology-type work. My first job out of grad school, I worked as an aquatic biologist, sampling streams and doing ecological assessments.

11
00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.419
Kate: And I…

12
00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:54.789
Kate: was not necessarily very happy at my firm, and I loved the fieldwork, but then when winter hit, you just had to analyze spreadsheets and write reports, and…

13
00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:55.400
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

14
00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:08.350
Kate: I had a friend who said, hey, I talked to this guy, I'm getting a job, with this guy who is, has been designing on-site wastewater systems for a long time, and he's looking to sell his business.

15
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:23.690
Kate: And so I interviewed, I got the job, my friend actually was like, oh, hey, I actually decided to go this other direction and take a job elsewhere. But it was this opportunity to do engineering, and…

16
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:40.869
Kate: in the ecology space, I felt like we wrote a lot of reports, and I never saw anything happen. And so it was really cool for me to go out to a project, go out to the field, see a problem, create a solution, and then see it implemented.

17
00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:53.999
Kate: And I just really enjoyed that. And I, you know, I'm out in the field all year long, and I feel like I'm preventing the environmental problems rather than just assessing them once they have happened.

18
00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:00.910
Ashley Christian: Yeah, I could see how that would bring a ton of fulfillment, to be able to see the whole process from beginning to end on that.

19
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:20.190
Kate: Yeah, yeah, so it's been a really… it's not something that I had thought was gonna be my path. I actually, in undergrad, I worked at a water lab for a while in Missouri, then I sampled lagoons in rural Missouri and analyzed those samples in a water lab, and that was the first time I actually realized

20
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.670
Kate: Or thought about where our wastewater went.

21
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:28.769
Kate: And so I did have a little bit of background with being familiar with on-site wastewater.

22
00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:35.870
Ashley Christian: Yeah, that's really interesting. So, what does a normal week for you look like now, in your career?

23
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:50.669
Kate: Sure, so, I have, another designer that works with me, and so we work together, splitting up the work, but we will get calls, we'll talk to clients, talk about their situation, provide proposals.

24
00:03:50.670 --> 00:04:06.979
Kate: And then schedule site visits. So we'll go out, meet with an excavator, we'll do a site assessment, dig soil test holes, take elevations, map out where repairs or, you know, on a new build where components will go.

25
00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:28.780
Kate: Come back to the office, complete the design, and then send that out for the client to submit for permitting and to installers that we work with to get bids. We also make site visits during installation to inspect the installation of the system, so we can write a final letter and provide record drawings to the county.

26
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:36.530
Ashley Christian: And are you working more with the excavator and installers, or more with the homeowners?

27
00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:49.210
Kate: We like the homeowner to be our client, because it's their property, and it's their product. We do work very closely with excavators, and a lot of times, that's who gets called first.

28
00:04:49.210 --> 00:05:07.180
Kate: So, they'll refer us to those clients, and we'll work closely with them. We like to meet them out on site so we can see those test pits excavated. That way, if we see something that's, you know, suboptimal, we could move to a different place on the site, or,

29
00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:25.439
Kate: In Colorado, we recently, in our new reg change, we worked really hard, Colorado professionals and on-site wastewater, to try to close the gap, because a lot of counties were requiring us to dig these 8-foot-deep holes and leave them open for the county to be able to look at at the later time.

30
00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:31.709
Kate: And it could be a month between when I get out on site and the county actually gets on site.

31
00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:46.109
Kate: It could be longer. Some of the counties take up to 8 weeks to permit a design, and so leaving these test holes open seemed like a really huge hazard and a liability for myself, for the excavator, for the homeowner, for the public. So…

32
00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:53.399
Kate: It's nice to be out there, they can dig the hole, and they can backfill it, and that way we're not leaving these holes open as a hazard.

33
00:05:53.910 --> 00:06:01.709
Ashley Christian: How do you decide between what type of system that you're gonna use? What are some of the major deciding factors you use?

34
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.280
Kate: So we like to go to simplest to more complicated, so…

35
00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:13.250
Kate: If we have the space, if we have good soil, we will do a very simple gravity flow

36
00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:23.480
Kate: type system. Unfortunately, we… we have density, you know, growing here in Colorado, and so a lot of times.

37
00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:39.370
Kate: We'll have either really poor clay soils, small lots in the mountains, we'll have rock soil. So we'll have these soil conditions that make us go to either a sand filter or sometimes higher level treatment.

38
00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:43.530
Kate: So it's really site-specific, and soil-specific.

39
00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:50.549
Ashley Christian: Yeah. And then you also do commercial systems, the design for that as well, which I know is…

40
00:06:50.740 --> 00:07:03.869
Ashley Christian: a whole different situation than your residential. And then also sometimes you have to look at, is it a short-term rental, where there's just a lot of people in and out? Is it a daycare? Is there things where it's just high use?

41
00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:10.970
Ashley Christian: So explain to us the… some of the big, major differences in designing a system between commercial and residential.

42
00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:20.940
Kate: Sure, so I would say we're actually fortunate that our code addresses the strength of the wastewater. So, for a very long time, everything was just based on flows.

43
00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:34.789
Kate: And so now our code says you cannot apply high-strength wastewater to the soil without pre-treatment, which is great, because if you put high-strength wastewater out to soil, it's not going to last very long.

44
00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:59.749
Kate: So we need to look at the use, and so it's really difficult. If we've got an existing facility, we can do wastewater sampling and get a good idea of the strength. Otherwise, we're looking at regulatory tables that aren't as accurate. But the most important thing is convincing that owner that being honest with you is to their benefit. So, sometimes people may think that if they tell us

45
00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:03.060
Kate: what they're actually doing, they're gonna get a more expensive system.

46
00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:17.039
Kate: And so, we get sometimes where they're not being honest about their use, and that's not going to benefit them, because maybe their system will be less expensive up front, but it's not going to work for the long run. Yeah.

47
00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:30.610
Kate: So, that's one of our challenges. We recently had a residential home that we designed as a residential home, and right after the system was installed, the owner went in for a short-term rental permit.

48
00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:46.080
Kate: And in Colorado, after 3 bedrooms, you only have to design for one person per bedroom. And this person wanted the short-term rental for more people, and then he was very upset, but he never disclosed to us that he was going to use it as a short-term rental.

49
00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.639
Kate: Because he wanted it designed as a residential system.

50
00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:51.630
Ashley Christian: Okay, to save money, so did.

51
00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:51.990
Kate: Yeah.

52
00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:57.249
Ashley Christian: Not get his short-term rental approved because his system couldn't handle it.

53
00:08:57.250 --> 00:09:01.740
Kate: Yep, and now he's looking at adding on to a brand new system.

54
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.599
Kate: It just got put in the ground. Yeah.

55
00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:14.550
Kate: So, trying… trying to educate people up front, a lot of times, owners will want to tell you that they're not using any water, and they're not putting any

56
00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:18.510
Kate: And, you know.

57
00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:30.219
Kate: I think having a survey for them that they sign, is really helpful, because, and educating them, that it's really important that we know

58
00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:41.519
Kate: You know, this isn't just an office building, it's going to be a restaurant. That's a very different use, very different flows and strengths, and we have to be able to know what we're treating in order to provide a solution.

59
00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:42.290
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

60
00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:48.479
Ashley Christian: That makes a lot of sense. What is the most complex system you've designed?

61
00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:53.600
Kate: I would say probably…

62
00:09:54.610 --> 00:10:06.470
Kate: There's a couple of them, and I would say probably there was one up in Boulder County for a, residential treatment facility, so kind of a…

63
00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:11.350
Kate: I don't know the right word, but inpatient-type facility.

64
00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:12.090
Ashley Christian: Okay.

65
00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:16.950
Kate: And it had been an old hotel, and…

66
00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:27.710
Kate: There is a… a road that goes… winds through the mountain, and there's a river on one side, and then this site is up on the hill, and then the mountain just goes straight up.

67
00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:41.050
Kate: And the existing system was just some tanks with a soil treatment area that was installed in the right-of-way, and then CDOT put a bike path over it.

68
00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.170
Ashley Christian: Okay.

69
00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:59.909
Kate: And so they needed a new system, and so not only did we need to be able to get components in, but we couldn't work in that right-of-way, where all the access was and where existing things were. And so, we ended up putting in septic tanks.

70
00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:15.489
Kate: right outside of the right-of-way, we could still gravity flow. They had an old grease trap that was like a… looked like a shoebox underneath the porch, so we had to eliminate that, put in a new grease interceptor tank, new tanks.

71
00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.500
Kate: treatment,

72
00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:31.659
Kate: And then we pumped up and ended up putting the drain field or soil treatment area under the parking area up above. It had to be traffic graded for fire truck access.

73
00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:32.320
Ashley Christian: Ow.

74
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:42.010
Kate: And then, because of the space, they actually had to chisel away part of the mountain to extend that area to get enough space up there.

75
00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:53.100
Kate: So it was pretty costly, but the other option was to build a road up the mountain and try to pump it up farther up the mountain, because all their land was…

76
00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.400
Kate: Was way on the top of the mountain.

77
00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:57.309
Ashley Christian: Wow, that does sound expensive.

78
00:11:57.310 --> 00:11:57.920
Kate: It was…

79
00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:21.569
Kate: It was very expensive. We were very lucky we had great excavators that we have a great working relationship with. Even getting sand, because when they excavate out that rock, they have to bring in treatment sand. The access in and out of there, on the curve, with the bike path, and getting sand trucks in and out of there, every part of it. And because they couldn't set those concrete tanks.

80
00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:39.599
Kate: We couldn't get the tankage required in plastic tanks. They couldn't sit on the road to set those tanks without blocking a road of that highway, which is very busy, so they ended up craning the tanks over the building to set those tanks. Wow.

81
00:12:39.660 --> 00:13:01.500
Kate: So it was incredible, and really, we were fortunate to be working with such great contractors, because not everyone could have done that job. And, you know, I understand that engineers can make things happen on paper that can't happen in real life, and so we were fortunate to have some really great excavators here in Colorado that can implement those solutions.

82
00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:02.110
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

83
00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:06.860
Ashley Christian: When you talked about having a great relationship with the excavators.

84
00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:25.980
Ashley Christian: Can you explain more what kind of things make… what qualities make a great relationship with an excavator? Because we have a lot of excavators, installers who listen, and so what kind of things really help the relationship work out really well? What kind of things are a deterrent to working well together?

85
00:13:27.070 --> 00:13:30.179
Kate: But I always say, I'd much rather you…

86
00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:48.419
Kate: call me ahead of time with any question you have. I would much rather field those calls, have those conversations, than to get out on site and someone's just done whatever they wanted, or what they may have thought. We try to provide very clear, detailed plans with elevations.

87
00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:51.059
Kate: But also, you know, you…

88
00:13:51.060 --> 00:14:14.070
Kate: especially with repairs, you encounter things in the field that just weren't anticipated during design, so having that communication is crucial. We are also always trying to be as responsive as possible. So I understand if you call for an inspection, and I can't get out there for a couple days, you have machinery sitting on site that's costing you money.

89
00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:18.630
Kate: So that respect for each other's time and,

90
00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:22.910
Kate: Yeah, ask… I say asking for permission rather than forgiveness.

91
00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:23.580
Ashley Christian: Right?

92
00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:30.519
Kate: Because there's nothing worse than showing up on site, and everything's just different than what you designed, and you're confused.

93
00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:43.889
Ashley Christian: Yeah, that's great. So, lots of good communication, and I love that just respecting each other's, you know, profession and how you work together goes a really long way, that respect, I'm sure.

94
00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:54.400
Kate: Yeah, and I have learned so much from installers, because I'm not the one physically making this happen, so if an installer says, hey, Kate, when you do it this way, it's really difficult for us.

95
00:14:54.400 --> 00:15:07.950
Kate: If you do it this way, this is a lot easier for us to implement. You know, also, operation and maintenance folks, you know, they'll say, hey, when you configure your effluent filter this way, it's really hard for us to pull it.

96
00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:17.749
Kate: If you, you know, those sorts of things, because if you make things difficult to maintain, it just won't get done. Yeah. So, listening to the people that are actually doing those jobs, and

97
00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:25.470
Kate: And respecting that, and trying to incorporate it so that it happens up front, and they don't have to retrofit or fix things at the tail end.

98
00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:40.409
Ashley Christian: What are some of the, like, please get a little technical here. What are some of the things that you've changed in the way that you design systems so that it's more doable for the, the maintenance providers and for the installers?

99
00:15:41.180 --> 00:16:00.790
Kate: Yeah, and I think, some of these things we've… we implemented, and we actually, worked really closely with the state when they were doing their regulation change to try to get these things incorporated in regulation as well. That's great. So things like risers to grade are now required in our regulation, which is great.

100
00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:13.150
Kate: We have external splice boxes now in our risers. There's no longer any splice boxes allowed inside the tank risers. They have to be on the outside, so that makes it, you know.

101
00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:31.429
Kate: a lot easier to work in that riser with other things. It also is less corrosive environment. We have always extended all handles, effluent filters, float tree handles up within 6 inches of the lid of the riser, so that you can reach your hand in there without

102
00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:36.289
Kate: Having to, you know, lay on your belly to reach down and grab something out of the tank.

103
00:16:38.670 --> 00:16:53.640
Kate: doing, you know, looking at the cords, having the cords neatly wrapped in that pump riser and attached to the side. Those, float trees are another thing. You know, it used to be people would just kind of attach

104
00:16:53.700 --> 00:17:06.840
Kate: floats to the discharge pipe that comes from the pump, and if you have a separate float tree, you can actually pull that out and test things without having to reach way down into a tank.

105
00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:26.709
Kate: We like to keep everything really shallow for access, so, that was another thing that was implemented into our reg, is that top of new tanks can't be deeper than 4 feet to the top of tank. On repairs, we struggle with that because people did some really strange things back in the day, made things really deep.

106
00:17:26.869 --> 00:17:37.649
Kate: But then coordinating with a builder. If you're working with a builder, emphasizing that the stub out from the house needs to be shallow so we can keep our tank shallow.

107
00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:47.260
Kate: on a flat site. Locating tanks in our designs within a range of a parking area so that pumpers can actually pump those tanks.

108
00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:48.250
Ashley Christian: That's smart.

109
00:17:48.250 --> 00:18:02.360
Kate: So we like to say 100 feet horizontally, 10 feet vertically is what we're aiming for as our max for those tanks. And some of our mountain sites, we can't achieve that, but keeping that in mind, the maintenance in mind, so that

110
00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:09.030
Kate: You know, in the future, someone's not having to haul 200 feet of pipe out there to pump that tank.

111
00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:23.950
Kate: Also in our soil treatment areas, we have observation pipes that are present so that you can actually look at the condition of those soil treatment areas. We have, flushing valves required on all our pressure laterals.

112
00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:30.129
Kate: So you can actually flush those laterals, and those have access at grade.

113
00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:38.020
Kate: Sometimes, depending on the site and the complexity, we'll use tracing wire on underground pipes, so you can locate it in the future.

114
00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:38.630
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

115
00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:57.049
Kate: So yeah, just a lot of those things to make things accessible, maintainable. Line-of-site control panels from our pump tanks to the control panel, so that you could… one person can easily do maintenance, and then the height of that control panel.

116
00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:04.879
Kate: I think our new reg says it has to be at least 30 inches, but we want, you know, probably at least 4 feet to that control panel, because

117
00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:13.510
Kate: once again, we're in Colorado, and we don't want that control panel covered in snow, we want to fix the cold, so we can do our maintenance.

118
00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:20.430
Ashley Christian: So, have you gotten all of these changes, have all been put into regulations now? Or just most of them?

119
00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:30.679
Kate: Most of them, regulation still doesn't call for tracing wire, but all these accessible things that grade and… and that sort of thing,

120
00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:32.910
Kate: Are part of regulation now.

121
00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:49.849
Ashley Christian: And you were able to help influence that because of your work at the state level, with the state association, and then also with NARA, or how did that work when you were involved in, sort of, you know, helping to bring this about for the betterment of everybody?

122
00:19:50.110 --> 00:20:13.950
Kate: Sure, yeah, so with Colorado Professionals and on-state wastewater, we always try to be really involved with any regular revision at the state level. Once the state adopts it, each county gets to adopt their version that's at least as stringent. So we also, try to be involved in those processes when we're able to be. Some of the counties are, ask for more input than others.

123
00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:28.699
Kate: And then I think just my relationship working with counties and regulators, I feel as though if you do good work, those regulators use you as a resource. So giving… being able to give them feedback.

124
00:20:28.910 --> 00:20:33.250
Kate: And be involved in those processes, I think, is really important.

125
00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:34.120
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

126
00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:49.220
Ashley Christian: And it… I… I love what I'm hearing, too, is you're saying that you learned these things from the maintenance providers and the excavators, and hearing their feedback, and then you were able to take that also with your relationship with the regulators.

127
00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:55.519
Ashley Christian: and create something that's better for everybody. So I love that you've been able to be the bridge between that.

128
00:20:55.520 --> 00:21:02.079
Ashley Christian: And I'm sure Colorado is much better for it, although you still do have all the old systems that…

129
00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:05.639
Ashley Christian: Folks have to deal with that aren't up to regulation.

130
00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:13.110
Kate: Yeah, and so that's another thing that, Colorado was able to do is implement a title transfer inspection program.

131
00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:13.850
Ashley Christian: Okay.

132
00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:24.909
Kate: Unfortunately, due to our political environment, it's an opt-in, opt-out program. So the counties can choose whether or not they wanted to adopt that portion of the regulation.

133
00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:25.680
Ashley Christian: Okay.

134
00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:47.730
Kate: So we have a lot of counties who have adopted that, and I think it's been wonderful. We are seeing a lot of systems that are in need of repair, are caught during that process when money is available during the property transfer. And I think that even some counties that don't require it, I think realtors that maybe work in multiple counties and

135
00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:55.249
Kate: you know, they may say, hey, in this county, it's required. Even though it's not required in this county, I'm gonna let my client know it should be important.

136
00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:56.790
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

137
00:21:56.920 --> 00:22:02.060
Ashley Christian: Yeah, it's important to have the… those records where… where everyone can… it follows the…

138
00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:09.220
Ashley Christian: follows the property, so that, you know, I have that data and what happened and everything.

139
00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:15.509
Kate: Yeah, and it's, you know, if you're buying a brand new house, and you're putting a good chunk of your money towards a down payment.

140
00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:31.120
Kate: it's really a bummer if you move in, and a couple days later, there's sewage on the ground, you know? It really is the time to find some of these problems, and the money's there during that transaction to get that situation remedied.

141
00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:49.380
Ashley Christian: So, when you… are you getting calls from, providers when a system fails? Even if it's not your system, or do you more get those calls when it's just your system? How does that work? Kind of that feedback loop when things don't go right on the back end of it?

142
00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:56.829
Kate: Yeah, so I get a lot of calls. We… a lot of the failures that we see are older systems.

143
00:22:56.830 --> 00:22:57.160
Ashley Christian: Yeah.

144
00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:12.389
Kate: So, you know, you look at them, you're like, well, this has probably lived its life. Right. There's also pockets in Colorado, where maybe there was terrible clay soil, but a builder came through and somehow had a soil scientist who

145
